<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Ruby series</th>
      {%- for base_image in base_images %}
        <th>{{ base_image }}</th>
      {%- endfor %}
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {%- for row in rows %}
      <tr{% if row.style %} style="{{ row.style }}"{% endif %}>
        <td>{{ row.major_minor }}{{ row.label }}</td>
        {%- for version in row.versions %}
          <td>{{ version or "-" }}</td>
        {%- endfor %}
      </tr>
    {%- endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>

> warning
> Rows shaded yellow are in security-only maintenance upstream, where Ruby Core ships security fixes only. Rows shaded red are end of life (EOL), receive no further updates, and fall outside the Heroku Ruby support policy. We recommend running the latest patch of a Ruby series that Ruby Core actively supports.

To use a Ruby version, specify it in your `Gemfile`. For example:

```ruby
# Gemfile
ruby '{{ example }}'
```

Then, with that Ruby version installed locally, update your `Gemfile.lock` by running:

```term
$ bundle update --ruby
```
